        <body>
            <div xml:id="d477">

                <pb facs="0627" n="313" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg313"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 300.115/340</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate General</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                        ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        notAfter="1914-09-17T16:30:00-05:00" notBefore="1914-09-17T00:00:00+00:00"
                            ><hi rendition="#italic">undated</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Received September 17, 1914, 4:30 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>In proposing to release certain American goods procurator general informs me
                    order will be made: “On payment of any sum which may be due in respect of
                    freight.” As goods are not landed at ports to which originally consigned,
                    payment of freight to London necessarily adds to shippers’ losses. Does
                    Department desire me to urge release of neutral goods free of all charges?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
